---
<LegalLayout
  kind="privacy"
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How the Catbird app and catbird.blue handle your data: what stays on your device, what Catbird’s servers keep, who else is involved, and the choices you have."
  path="/privacy"
  lede={`How the Catbird app for iPhone and iPad (iOS ${MIN_IOS} or later) and this website handle your data.`}
  toc={toc}
  extras={extras}
  draftDetail="This privacy policy is being reviewed and does not apply yet."
>
  <section class="legal-summary" aria-labelledby="in-short">
    <h2 id="in-short">In short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>You sign in on your own account server, in your browser. Catbird never sees your password.</li>
      <li>Catbird’s server, called Nest, keeps your sign-in tokens (encrypted) and relays most of what the app does with your account, including Bluesky direct messages.</li>
      <li>If you turn on notifications, Nest stores your device token and notification settings. Alerts can include up to 160 characters of public post text and are delivered through Apple.</li>
      <li>Crash and performance reports go to Sentry in the United States and can include your account ID. There’s no setting to turn them off yet.</li>
      <li>You can sign out, change notification and embed settings, and ask for the data on Catbird’s servers to be deleted. See <a href="#choices">your choices</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <h2 id="who-we-are">Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    Catbird is an independent, open-source app for using Bluesky on iPhone and iPad. This policy covers the Catbird app, the servers Catbird runs to make the app work, and this website, catbird.blue. When it says “we” or “Catbird”, it means the person responsible for Catbird.
  </p>
  <p>
    The person responsible for your data (the data controller) is{' '}
    {LEGAL.controllerName ? <strong>{LEGAL.controllerName}</strong> : <Pending label="legal name" />}
    {LEGAL.postalAddress ? <>, {LEGAL.postalAddress}</> : <>, at <Pending label="postal address" /></>}.
    You can reach us using the details in <a href="#contact">Contact</a>.
  </p>
  <p>Catbird is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or officially connected to Bluesky Social, PBC.</p>

  <h2 id="what-catbird-is">What Catbird is and isn’t</h2>
  <p>
    Catbird is an app for the AT Protocol, the open network Bluesky runs on. Your account, posts, follows, likes and lists live on your account’s server (its <abbr title="Personal Data Server">PDS</abbr>), which for most people is run by Bluesky, and Bluesky direct messages are kept by Bluesky’s chat service. They don’t live with Catbird. Anything you post publicly is public on the whole network, whichever app you use.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your account server’s own privacy policy covers what it does with your account. For accounts hosted by Bluesky, that’s <a href={BSKY_PRIVACY}>Bluesky’s Privacy Policy</a>. Deleting Catbird doesn’t delete your account.
  </p>
  <p>
    This policy describes Catbird 1.0. If a future version adds features that handle data differently, we’ll update this policy before that version ships.
  </p>

  <h2 id="on-your-device">Data on your device</h2>
  <h3>Sign-in</h3>
  <p>
    Your iPhone or iPad’s Keychain stores a session ID that lets the app talk to Catbird’s server, an account record with your account ID (your <abbr title="Decentralized Identifier">DID</abbr>) and handle, and short-lived details while a sign-in is in progress. These items are kept on this device only, are available after you first unlock the device, and aren’t synced to iCloud Keychain. Your Bluesky sign-in tokens are not stored on your device; Catbird’s server holds them (see <a href="#catbird-servers">Data on Catbird’s servers</a>).
  </p>
  <h3>Local storage</h3>
  <p>
    The app keeps a local database with cached feed posts, your place in each feed, your settings and your drafts, so it opens quickly. It isn’t synced to iCloud. Deleting the app removes it. Keychain items can remain on a device after the app is deleted.
  </p>
  <h3>Spotlight and Siri</h3>
  <p>
    Posts from the feed pages you load, and profiles you view, are added to your device’s Spotlight index so you can find them in Search and so Siri can understand what’s on screen. The index stays on your device and holds up to 2,000 recent items.
  </p>
  <h3>Translation</h3>
  <p>
    Translating a post uses Apple’s Translation framework. Catbird doesn’t send the text to its own servers or to another translation service.
  </p>
  <h3>Age</h3>
  <p>
    Catbird asks iOS whether age-related rules apply where you are. It doesn’t ask for your age or age range, and it doesn’t store one.
  </p>
  <h3>Diagnostics that stay on your device</h3>
  <p>
    Daily performance summaries from iOS (MetricKit) are saved inside the app and aren’t uploaded. The app’s system log leaves your device only if you share it yourself from Settings.
  </p>
  <h3>Location</h3>
  <p>Catbird doesn’t use location services.</p>

  <h2 id="catbird-servers">Data on Catbird’s servers</h2>
  <p>
    Catbird runs a server called Nest at <code>api.catbird.blue</code>. It handles sign-in and relays the app’s requests to your account server. Nest and Catbird’s notification service are hosted by Netcup.
  </p>

  <h3>Your sign-in session</h3>
  <p>
    When you sign in, the app opens your account server’s sign-in page in your browser. Your password goes only to that server. After you approve Catbird, Nest receives and keeps your sign-in tokens (an access token and a refresh token), the private key used to prove those tokens belong to Catbird’s session, the permissions you granted, your DID, your handle and your account server’s address.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nest encrypts each of these records (AES-256-GCM) before storing it. Your DID appears unencrypted in the names of these stored records so Nest can find them. A session ends 30 days after it was last used, and each use extends it.
  </p>
  <p>
    At sign-in, Catbird asks for full access to your account, including Bluesky direct messages, because it’s a complete Bluesky app. It asks for extra permissions only when you use a feature that needs them, such as changing your handle, managing your email address or deactivating your account. Catbird never asks for permission to delete your account.
  </p>

  <h3>Requests Nest relays</h3>
  <p>
    Most of what the app does with your account goes through Nest, which forwards it to your account server. That includes loading feeds and profiles, posting, following, uploading images (up to about 10 MB), reporting content, and Bluesky direct messages. Some requests go from your device straight to other services; see <a href="#third-parties">Other services</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nest sees your IP address when the app connects. It uses it in memory to limit request rates and doesn’t pass it on to your account server.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nest’s logs record each request’s type and its query parameters (for example, a search term or an account ID), and your DID when you sign in or out (with your handle at sign-in). The contents of what you post or send are logged only as field names and types, not their values. How long these logs are kept is not yet defined.
  </p>

  <h3>Settings Nest keeps</h3>
  <p>Nest also stores the following, linked to your DID. These records are not encrypted in Catbird’s database.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your push notification registration and settings (see <a href="#push-notifications">Push notifications</a>).</li>
    <li>Threads you mute. Nest reads your mute request as it passes through, so it can stop notifications for that thread.</li>
    <li>Bluesky direct-message conversations you mute, and, while the chat screen is open, a signal every 60 seconds that you’re viewing your messages.</li>
    <li>
      A short-lived check of your relationship with the account behind a notification (both DIDs, its display name and whether you block or mute it), so you aren’t notified about accounts you’ve blocked or muted. Each check is deleted after 1 hour.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>GIFs and YouTube</h3>
  <p>
    GIF searches go to Catbird’s server at <code>catbird.blue</code>, which passes them to Klipy, the GIF service. If you allow YouTube videos to play, they play inside a Catbird page at <code>youtube.catbird.blue</code> that loads YouTube’s player, so that server sees the video ID and your IP address.
  </p>

  <h3>What signing out does</h3>
  <p>
    Signing out deletes your session from Nest and asks your account server to revoke Catbird’s tokens. If that request fails, Nest keeps a retry record containing the session ID for up to 7 days. Signing out also marks your device as no longer registered for notifications.
  </p>
  <p>
    Signing out does not delete your notification registration record, notification settings, activity subscriptions, thread mutes, muted conversations, or older inactive device records (up to 20 per account). We delete these when you ask; see <a href="#deletion">Deleting your data</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="push-notifications">Push notifications</h2>
  <p>
    Catbird asks before sending notifications. If you allow them, the app registers your device with Nest, not with Bluesky. Nest stores your DID, your device’s push token with the technical details Apple needs to deliver to it, the notification types you’ve chosen, and any accounts you’ve asked to be notified about when they post (activity subscriptions).
  </p>
  <p>
    Catbird’s notification service reads Bluesky’s public stream of new posts, likes, follows and other activity, and picks out events that concern you. It caches public post text for up to 60 minutes and account identity details for up to 24 hours. Each pending notification is deleted once it’s delivered.
  </p>
  <p>
    Notifications are delivered by Apple’s push notification service. An alert can include the other account’s display name and, for mentions, replies, quotes and new posts from accounts you’ve subscribed to, up to 160 characters of the public post’s text, along with the account IDs and post links the app needs to open it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Notifications for Bluesky direct messages aren’t turned on yet. Registering for notifications does record your DID and account server address in a list Nest would use for message notifications. Before message notifications are turned on, this policy will be updated to explain how they work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="crash-reports">Crash and performance reports</h2>
  <p>
    The app sends crash and performance reports to Sentry, a monitoring service, at its United States location. Reporting is on by default, and there is no setting in Catbird to turn it off today.
  </p>
  <p>Reports include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>crash reports and reports of the app freezing;</li>
    <li>a sample of performance measurements: 5% of performance traces and 2% of profiles;</li>
    <li>sign-out, account-switching and sign-in error events, which include your full DID;</li>
    <li>recent log messages from the app’s networking code. Query values are removed from the web addresses it logs, but other log text is sent as written;</li>
    <li>the app version, and other technical details Sentry’s software attaches by default.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>How long Sentry keeps reports is not yet defined. We use these reports only to find and fix problems.</p>

  <h2 id="third-parties">Other services involved</h2>
  <p>
    Besides Catbird’s own servers, these services receive data when you use Catbird. Each handles it under its own privacy policy.
  </p>
  <table class="legal-table">
    <caption class="visually-hidden">Services that receive data when you use Catbird</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Service</th><th scope="col">When</th><th scope="col">What it receives</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Your account server (PDS), usually Bluesky’s</th>
        <td data-label="When">When you sign in in your browser, and for every request Nest relays</td>
        <td data-label="What">Your sign-in and everything you do with your account</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Bluesky’s app services, moderation service, and labelers you subscribe to</th>
        <td data-label="When">When your account server passes on feed, profile, message and report requests</td>
        <td data-label="What">Those requests. Reports go to Bluesky’s moderation service or to the labeler you choose.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Bluesky’s media servers</th>
        <td data-label="When">When you view images and videos</td>
        <td data-label="What">Your IP address and which media you load</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row"><code>video.bsky.app</code></th>
        <td data-label="When">When you post a video</td>
        <td data-label="What">The video and a short-lived token that authorizes the upload</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row"><code>cardyb.bsky.app</code></th>
        <td data-label="When">When you add a link to a new post</td>
        <td data-label="What">The link, to build its preview card</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row"><code>public.api.bsky.app</code></th>
        <td data-label="When">When you open a starter pack or Bluesky link before signing in</td>
        <td data-label="What">The starter pack or handle being looked up</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row"><code>plc.directory</code> and account domains</th>
        <td data-label="When">When the app looks up an account’s identity</td>
        <td data-label="What">The account ID being looked up</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Klipy, Tenor and GIPHY</th>
        <td data-label="When">When you search for or view GIFs</td>
        <td data-label="What">Klipy receives your GIF searches through Catbird’s server. GIF images load directly from Klipy, or from Tenor or GIPHY for older GIFs, so those services see your IP address.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Embedded media: YouTube, Vimeo, Twitch, Spotify, Apple Music, SoundCloud, GIPHY, Tenor, Klipy, Flickr and Bandcamp</th>
        <td data-label="When">Only after you allow that provider. Each starts as Ask Before Playing.</td>
        <td data-label="What">Your IP address and what you play</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Apple</th>
        <td data-label="When">Notifications, Siri and Shortcuts, Spotlight, translation, Apple Intelligence, TestFlight and the App Store</td>
        <td data-label="What">Notification contents (see <a href="#push-notifications">Push notifications</a>) and what Apple’s own services need</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Sentry</th>
        <td data-label="When">When the app sends a crash or performance report</td>
        <td data-label="What">See <a href="#crash-reports">Crash and performance reports</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Cloudflare</th>
        <td data-label="When">For every connection to <code>catbird.blue</code> and its subdomains</td>
        <td data-label="What">Your IP address and the requests passing through its network</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Netcup</th>
        <td data-label="When">Always, as the host of Catbird’s servers</td>
        <td data-label="What">Stores the data described in <a href="#catbird-servers">Data on Catbird’s servers</a></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="on-device-ai">On-device AI</h2>
  <p>
    Ask Catbird, Generate Alt Text, Smart Filters, post classification and topic summaries use Apple’s on-device language model. They need iOS 26 and Apple Intelligence. Apple’s Private Cloud Compute is turned off in Catbird, and no third-party AI service is used, so your prompts and the posts these features read are processed on your device.
  </p>
  <p>
    When Ask Catbird needs Bluesky data, it fetches it through Nest like any other request. It proposes actions, and nothing happens until you confirm.
  </p>

  <h2 id="website">Visiting this website</h2>
  <p>
    catbird.blue doesn’t use cookies, analytics or tracking scripts, and your browser loads everything from catbird.blue itself. If you choose a theme, your choice is saved in your browser’s local storage and never sent to us. Because the site doesn’t track you, it works the same whether or not your browser sends a Do Not Track signal.
  </p>
  <p>
    Like any website, the servers that deliver it (Cloudflare and Catbird’s web server) receive your IP address, the page you asked for, the time and your browser’s description of itself, and may log them. How long these logs are kept is not yet defined.
  </p>

  <h2 id="retention">How long data is kept</h2>
  <table class="legal-table">
    <caption class="visually-hidden">Retention periods for data Catbird handles</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Data</th><th scope="col">Where</th><th scope="col">How long</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Sign-in session and tokens</th>
        <td data-label="Where">Nest</td>
        <td data-label="How long">Until you sign out, or 30 days after last use</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Sign-in in progress</th>
        <td data-label="Where">Nest</td>
        <td data-label="How long">10 minutes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Token revocation retry (only if revocation fails at sign-out)</th>
        <td data-label="Where">Nest</td>
        <td data-label="How long">Up to 7 days</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Markers for ended sessions (no account details)</th>
        <td data-label="Where">Nest</td>
        <td data-label="How long">90 days</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Notification registration, settings, activity subscriptions, thread mutes and muted conversations</th>
        <td data-label="Where">Nest</td>
        <td data-label="How long">Until you ask us to delete them. Inactive device records beyond the 20 most recent are deleted.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Block and mute checks for notifications</th>
        <td data-label="Where">Nest</td>
        <td data-label="How long">Up to 1 hour</td>
      </tr>
